     bbaasseennaammee — extract the base portion of a pathname

SSYYNNOOPPSSIISS
     ##iinncclluuddee <<lliibbggeenn..hh>>

     _c_h_a_r _*
     bbaasseennaammee(_c_h_a_r _*_p_a_t_h);

DDEESSCCRRIIPPTTIIOONN
     The bbaasseennaammee() function returns the last component from the pathname
     pointed to by _p_a_t_h, deleting any trailing ‘/’ characters.

IIMMPPLLEEMMEENNTTAATTIIOONN NNOOTTEESS
     This implementation of bbaasseennaammee() uses the buffer provided by the caller
     to store the resulting pathname component.  Other vendor implementations
     may return a pointer to internal storage space instead.  The advantage of
     the former approach is that it ensures thread-safety, while also placing
     no upper limit on the supported length of the pathname.

RREETTUURRNN VVAALLUUEESS
     If _p_a_t_h consists entirely of ‘/’ characters, a pointer to the string "/"
     is returned.  If _p_a_t_h is a null pointer or the empty string, a pointer to
     the string "." is returned.  Otherwise, it returns a pointer to the last
     component of _p_a_t_h.

SSEEEE AALLSSOO
     basename(1), dirname(1), dirname(3)

SSTTAANNDDAARRDDSS
     The bbaasseennaammee() function conforms to X/Open Portability Guide Issue 4,
     Version 2 (“XPG4.2”).

HHIISSTTOORRYY
     The bbaasseennaammee() function first appeared in OpenBSD 2.2 and FreeBSD 4.2.

     In FreeBSD 12.0, this function was reimplemented to store its result in
     the provided input buffer.  There is no longer any need to use the
     bbaasseennaammee__rr() function.

